    "evidence_text": "Introduction and related work\tLanguage models can be optimized to recognize syntax and semantics with great accuracy .\n\nWe attempt to do this through a discriminator which can give feedback to\n\nLi et al.  use a discriminator for a diversity promoting objective.\n\nHowever, these studies do not focus solely on creative text.\n\nOur generator is a language model trained using backpropagation through time .\n\n$Sigmoid$ layer.\n\nWe utilize AWD-LSTM  and TransformerXL  based language models.\n\nFor model hyperparameters please to refer to Supplementary Section Table TABREF5.\n\nWe use three creative English datasets with distinct linguistic characteristics: (1) A corpus of 740 classical and contemporary English poems,\n\nThe mix of linguistic styles within this corpus offers the potential for interesting variation during the generation phase.\n\nWe use the same pre-processing as in earlier work , .\n\nlog-likelihood probabilities, as sampling has shown to produce better output quality .\n\nWe pick these values after experimentation with our validation set.\n\nTraining and output generation code can be found online.\n\nEvaluation and conclusion\tEvaluating creative generation tasks is both critical and complex .\n\nSample outputs for our model can be found on our website .\n\nfor each encoding method and Table TABREF6 shows our training parameters.",
